<h2>The route that defines Indian maize export</h2>
<p>Nepal isn't just India's largest maize market — at roughly USD 90 million in 2024, it's about two-thirds of India's total maize export volume and value, which makes it the route that effectively defines what "Indian maize export" means in practice. Everything about this trade, from routing to settlement, is shaped by the fact that Nepal shares a land border with northern India.</p>

<h2>Overland, not ocean freight</h2>
<p>Unlike almost every other product-market pair on this site, maize to Nepal is predominantly a road-and-rail trade, not a container-shipping one. Sourced from maize-growing states like Bihar, Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh, grain can reach Nepali buyers through border crossings in as little as 1–3 days — a fraction of the time and, often, the cost of any sea-freight alternative. Bulk vessel/rail loading is particularly practical here given the short distances and high volumes involved.</p>

<h2>INR acceptance removes a real layer of friction</h2>
<p>Indian Rupees circulate freely alongside the Nepali Rupee in Nepal, which sidesteps the currency-conversion complexity that shapes trade-finance decisions on almost every other route we cover. That's part of why TT and open-account terms are common here — the settlement friction that pushes other markets toward LC-heavy practices is largely absent.</p>

<h2>National supply dynamics land hardest here</h2>
<p>Because Nepal absorbs such a large share of India's total maize exports, any tightening in India's overall exportable surplus — whether from the ethanol-blending programme's demand or a policy adjustment — shows up in Nepal-route volumes more visibly than on any smaller route. See our <a href="/products/grains-rice/maize/">maize product page</a> for the fuller policy-volatility context that applies here.</p>

